00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:45,320
This is where, like the love of a parent for a child.

265
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:46,960
What's the kid ever gonna do for you?

266
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:49,980
Probably nothing, but I love this kid more than anything.

267
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:53,720
Or when you have the love of a child for an aged parent,

268
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:55,480
and when the parent dies, you get an inheritance,

269
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:56,940
and the parent is just a lot of work,

270
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:58,680
and they're not much fun anymore, and yet the kid

271
00:09:58,680 --> 00:10:00,880
keeps taking care of the parent and taking them

272
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:02,240
to the appointments.

273
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:03,660
And again, or some people are doing this

274
00:10:03,660 --> 00:10:05,520
because they don't want to be written out of the will.

275
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:08,080
But there's plenty of other people who know their parents

276
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:10,880
are just gonna give them their share, whatever they do,

277
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:13,680
but they just love their parents, and they feel devotion.

278
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:14,780
All right, so anyway.

279
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:18,000
There is, so altruism, there's also altruism

280
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,940
caring directly about others, but there seems to be

281
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:22,840
more to interpersonal motivation going on

282
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:25,960
than either true altruism, where you truly care

283
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:27,800
about someone, or total selfishness,

284
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:30,120
where you only care about yourself.

285
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:34,240
So one thing, when we treat people better

286
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:36,000
than selfishness would recommend,

287
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,280
it seems like the real reason is not really

288
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:42,480
that we care about them, but rather that we are concerned

289
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:45,460
that we treat them fairly.

290
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:47,700
So it seems to be about we must be fair

291
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:50,620
rather than that we actually care about them.

292
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:55,000
All right, so an example of this, so suppose that you are

293
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,280
at the beach with your child, and your kid goes

294
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,040
and loses his jacket, and then he's freezing.

295
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,960
The jacket gets washed away in the ocean.

296
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:02,960
So what will every parent do when they see

297
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:04,160
the freezing child?

298
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:06,600
The parent has a jacket too, hint, hint.

299
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:08,280
What does every parent do?

300
00:11:08,280 --> 00:11:10,400
Take off my jacket, give it to the child

301
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:11,520
so the child can be warm.

302
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:14,440
I would rather that I freeze than that my child

303
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:17,400
would freeze because I love the child.

304
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:18,960
It's like, yeah, but what for?

305
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:20,280
What's the kid ever gonna give back?

306
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:22,960
Is this so that he'll give you jackets later on?

307
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:25,600
No, nothing to do with that.

308
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:28,080
It's that I would rather personally suffer

309
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:31,320
and be freezing than watch my child be unhappy.

310
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,080
All right, so that's real love.

311
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:38,080
Now, now suppose, however, that there were a,

312
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:40,120
so that's, you know, the other year,

313
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:41,500
there was a stranger on the beach.

314
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:43,960
You both find, you go and find two jackets,

315
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:45,160
and you say, well, what's the fair thing to do?

316
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:46,760
Well, let's each get a jacket.

317
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:50,920
And then your friend goes and loses his jacket.

318
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:52,680
In that case, you're not so likely to go

319
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:55,200
and give him the jacket because, look,

320
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:57,300
he said, look, you cared about me enough

321
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:58,600
to give me a jacket the first time.

322
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:00,000
Why not do it again?

323
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,280
Because it wouldn't be fair for me

324
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,320
to have kept both jackets.

325
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:05,240
But after we divided up the jackets,

326
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:07,120
then it's your own tough luck, right?

327
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:09,120
And then it's your problem, right?

328
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:11,120
So, you know, it doesn't seem like you actually

329
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:12,760
really care about that person so much

330
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:14,920
as you care about you being a fair person.

331
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:18,160
This seems to have a lot to do with tipping as well.

332
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:21,120
So suppose that you found out that after you left

333
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:23,040
a nice tip for the waiter, that the waiter

334
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:24,480
accidentally went and dropped the money

335
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:25,520
down a storm drain.

336
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:28,440
Would you be inclined to go and give the waiter

337
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:31,360
a double tip the next time to go and help them out?

338
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:32,200
No.

339
00:12:32,200 --> 00:12:34,560
No, because look, I gave you what was fair.

340
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:37,200
I assume you received a certain reward.

341
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:40,640
But after that, I'm done.

342
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:42,200
Like, so that's one less.

343
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,780
But if you really cared about me,

344
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:46,240
you would give me the money because I need it

345
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:48,120
just as much as I did before.

346
00:12:48,120 --> 00:12:49,400
What is it really that I care about you?

347
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:51,160
I care about me treating you

348
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:53,520
in the socially acceptable way.

349
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:57,680
Which is very different from directly caring about you,

350
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:58,760
which is what you'd have for your kid.

351
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:01,280
For your kid, it's not fair that I don't have a jacket.

352
00:13:01,280 --> 00:13:03,000
Actually, it's really fair that you not have a jacket

353
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:04,640
because you lost your jacket, which I warned you

354
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:07,440
to not lose, but I love you, so I'm giving you

355
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:08,520
another jacket anyway.

356
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:11,640
But if the waiter would say, it's not fair

357
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:14,440
that you don't make up the tip, I need the tip more.

358
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:16,240
Or you can imagine a waiter saying, look,

359
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:17,520
you know how you're giving me some more money

360
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:19,240
than you legally have to, right?

361
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:21,760
Well, let me tell you how tough my life is.

362
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:23,880
And then maybe you'll give me more money.

363
00:13:26,040 --> 00:13:27,880
Now, not only are you not that likely to give him more money,

364
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:28,920
you might even not want to come back

365
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:30,360
to that restaurant anymore.

366
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,000
Because now that I've learned that the waiter

367
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:33,760
really needs the money, I don't want to have

368
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:35,520
anything to do with him.

369
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:37,200
It's a very unpleasant experience.

370
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:41,200
So anyway, we seem more concerned about how we treat people

371
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:44,000
who we directly interact with, and we pay more attention

372
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,600
to whether we behave fairly than the actual welfare

373
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:49,960
of others, which is of much less interest.

374
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:51,840
So going back to the experimental evidence,

375
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:54,320
remember ultimatum and dictator games?

376
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:56,080
So it's very common for the first mover

377
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:58,840
to share earnings with the second mover,

378
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,520
but very unusual to share earnings

379
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:02,480
with the next stranger that you meet.

380
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:04,200
What's the difference between sharing money

381
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:05,800
with the other person that's playing with you

382
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:07,720
in the ultimatum game, and sharing money

383
00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:10,800
with the first stranger that you meet in the hall?

384
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:13,720
In principle, they seem like they're both strangers, right?

385
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:15,720
But people feel differently because, look,

386
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,200
we're playing together.

387
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:19,440
And since we have this social bond,

388
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:21,520
then fairness rules kick in, and then I'm

389
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:23,160
supposed to share with you.

390
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:24,720
On the other hand, the person out there in the hall,

391
00:14:24,720 --> 00:14:25,760
I've never met that person.

392
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:26,440
He's a stranger.

393
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:28,160
I don't know him anything, so I don't have

394
00:14:28,160 --> 00:14:29,960
to go and give him anything.

395
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:31,680
That's the idea.

396
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:35,560
All right, so fairness suggests that you go and split the pie

397
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:37,680
evenly with your partner.

398
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:40,960
But genuine caring about mankind would actually

399
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:43,320
lead you to go and find out who needs this money the most,

400
00:14:43,320 --> 00:14:46,240
and then give the money to that person, which, again,

401
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:48,360
is very unusual.

402
00:14:48,360 --> 00:14:50,760
And of course, when you see someone going and giving money

403
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:53,360
to a person who seems to really need it,

404
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:57,960
is your reaction to say that's a super fair person?

405
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:00,600
Like when you see someone going and opening up their whole lot

406
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:03,560
and giving all the money to a homeless person.

407
00:15:03,560 --> 00:15:06,240
I've never seen this, actually, but I've read about it.

408
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:07,960
So when that happens, is your reaction

409
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:11,240
to say that person is so fair?

410
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:15,120
No, reaction is more like that person is really nice.

411
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:18,800
That person seems to actually like the homeless person,

412
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:21,960
to be concerned about the welfare of that homeless person.

413
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:24,640
Unless he's trying to impress someone who's watching.

414
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:26,120
Maybe that's what's going on.

415
00:15:26,120 --> 00:15:27,640
Might be your other reaction.

416
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:29,840
But again, the idea of fairness is

417
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:31,880
you have some kind of relationship with the person.

418
00:15:31,880 --> 00:15:33,360
There's some kind of social norm about how

419
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:34,760
you're supposed to split things.

420
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:37,880
You do, and once you do that, then you're done.

421
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:39,720
Whereas, of course, if you really care about someone,

422
00:15:39,720 --> 00:15:41,120
you're never done.

423
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:43,520
Because they're always having new problems, new needs,

424
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:45,040
new concerns, and if you care about them,

425
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:46,740
you're always going and looking into them

426
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:49,040
and trying to help them out.

427
00:15:49,040 --> 00:15:52,200
So questions about that?

428
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:55,160
So again, when you do tipping, while it's not selfish,

429
00:15:55,160 --> 00:15:56,560
it's not really altruism either.

430
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:59,600
It seems to be motivated by some kind of fairness norm.

431
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:02,160
And the fairness norm used to be that you tip 10%.

432
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:05,120
When I was a kid, it was 10%, and it was really easy to do it.

433
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:08,640
Then you get 15%, not so, not so, a little bit harder.

434
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:11,720
18%, very confusing.

435
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:14,600
So some people now make it 20%.

436
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:17,520
And of course, now notice one other thing.

437
00:16:17,520 --> 00:16:19,140
If you're traveling in another country,

438
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:22,960
people commonly ask, what do people tip around here,

439
00:16:22,960 --> 00:16:24,360
or do they?

440
00:16:24,360 --> 00:16:26,760
And whatever it is that is normal in that country,

441
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:30,000
people generally acclimate to pretty easily.

442
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,800
Because it's not that you really care about servers,

443
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:36,240
or that you say, well, in Japan, I don't care about them,

444
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:37,360
but in America, I do.

445
00:16:37,360 --> 00:16:40,320
Rather, say, well, in Japan, tipping is not customary.

446
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:43,280
So it's not unfair to fail to tip.

447
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:45,560
In fact, I've heard that if you try to tip in Japan,

448
00:16:45,560 --> 00:16:46,360
it's insulting.

449
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:47,440
Is that correct?

450
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:47,940
Yes?

451
00:16:47,940 --> 00:16:51,240
I used to live in Japan, and yeah, it's condescending.

452
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:51,920
Yes.

453
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:53,960
Will they still take the money, or will they pass it back

454
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:54,800
to you and be angry?

455
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:55,400
I never try.

456
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:55,900
Oh.

457
00:16:55,900 --> 00:16:58,880
So you ever see an American go and try tipping, and then?

458
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:01,080
So everybody now, sorry.

459
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:04,440
So when I'm in Europe, so the first day or two

460
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:05,740
I'm in a European country, that's

461
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:07,320
where it's very hard to adjust.

462
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:11,040
It's like, I'm just going to pay the bill, and then walk away.

463
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:11,840
It's kind of weird.

464
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:13,520
And then, especially when they say, well,

465
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:16,440
it's customary to sort of give whatever pocket change you have.

466
00:17:16,440 --> 00:17:18,000
Well, I don't have any pocket change.

467
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:19,000
What do I do?

468
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,400
I don't want people here to think

469
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:22,640
that I'm a big American jerk.

470
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:25,400
But then, after a few days, then you

471
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,320
start to feel, OK, this is what's normal here.

472
00:17:27,320 --> 00:17:30,200
OK, now I think I understand what people looks at.

473
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:31,620
Well, every now and then, there's

474
00:17:31,620 --> 00:17:32,920
someone who'll say, well, he's an American,

475
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:34,800
so he's supposed to do the American thing.

476
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:36,520
No, I thought we were in Germany,

477
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:37,680
and we do the German thing.

478
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:38,360
No, no, no.

479
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:41,240
Americans have to be American even here.

480
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:42,720
No.

481
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:43,440
All right.

482
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:44,920
So anyway, that's fairness.

483
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:50,680
Now, another motive that we see experimentally, as well as

484
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:52,200
seem to experience introspectively,

485
00:17:52,200 --> 00:17:55,120
is called vindictiveness.

486
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:56,240
Vindictiveness.

487
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,360
All right, so this is what we can see in the ultimatum game.

488
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:01,320
If you offer less than an even split,

489
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:04,640
there's a very good chance that the other person will cut off

490
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:10,120
his nose to spite his face and say, $4 out of 10,

491
00:18:10,120 --> 00:18:11,280
I don't think so.

492
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:13,200
How about no dollars for either of us?

493
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:17,040
Ha, you're stuck on that, greedy jerk.

494
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:19,200
All right, that is a pretty common reaction

495
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:22,680
to uneven splits.

496
00:18:22,680 --> 00:18:24,200
All right, now you may say, well,

497
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:25,040
but what do you care?

498
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:26,080
He's a complete stranger.

499
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:29,400
It's just a game, and you just gave up $4 to hurt someone

500
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:31,000
that you don't even know?

501
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,400
That's right.

502
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:37,800
A lot of people have this reaction to the experiment.

503
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:41,080
So the idea is that, especially when we're treated unfairly,

504
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:42,920
we think we've been treated unfairly,

505
00:18:42,920 --> 00:18:46,960
then we actually are worse than economists dangerously assume.

506
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:49,720
We then put negative weight on other people.

507
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:51,200
So normally, of course, economists

508
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:52,360
just say people are selfish.

509
00:18:52,360 --> 00:18:54,440
You don't put any positive weight on other people.

510
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:55,480
But there's experimental evidence

511
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:57,720
that we sometimes put negative weight on other people.

512
00:18:57,720 --> 00:19:00,440
Another way in which it would be really good if people were just

513
00:19:00,440 --> 00:19:02,840
regular old homo economicus, with regular old homo

514
00:19:02,840 --> 00:19:05,160
economicus, no one ever wants to go and wipe out

515
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:07,840
all the members of another group because what one member did.

516
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:10,560
No one wants to go and kill off other people.

517
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:14,520
It's just like, hey, well, what's done is done.

518
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:15,560
What can I do about it?

519
00:19:15,560 --> 00:19:18,520
You might want to go and punish the actual person who did it

520
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:21,360
because you want a reputation, but to go and just

521
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:24,920
punish a random person because you're like, well,

522
00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:26,160
